                                     "type": "text",                                                                                   
                                     "text": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on                                                 
                             /home/pst/Documents/vhl_librarian/virtual_hardware_laboratary/docs/vhl_library_readme.md:\n               
                             1\t# Virtual Hardware Laboratory (VHL) \ud83d\udd27\n     2\t\n     3\tA TypeScript-based                 
                             Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for tscircuit-driven hardware design. VHL enables LLM                 
                             agents to search, validate, and manage hardware components through a standardized API.\n                  
                             4\t\n     5\t## \ud83c\udfaf Overview\n     6\t\n     7\tVHL Library provides:\n     8\t\n                
                             9\t- **Component Management**: Add, validate, and store TSX components and KiCAD footprints\n             
                             10\t- **Library Search**: Search both local and global (tscircuit) component libraries\n                  
                             11\t- **Dual Transport**: Run locally via stdio or as a Docker service via HTTP\n    12\t-                
                             **Automatic Validation**: TypeScript compilation and dependency checking\n    13\t- **MCP                 
                             Protocol**: Standardized interface for AI agent integration\n    14\t\n    15\t## \u26a1                  
                             Quick Start\n    16\t\n    17\t### Local Development (stdio)\n    18\t\n    19\t```bash\n                 
                             20\t# Install dependencies\n    21\tpnpm install\n    22\t\n    23\t# Run the MCP server\n                
                             24\tpnpm run dev\n    25\t\n    26\t# Run tests\n    27\tpnpm test\n    28\t```\n    29\t\n               
                             30\t### Docker Deployment (HTTP)\n    31\t\n    32\t```bash\n    33\t# Using Docker Compose               
                             (Recommended)\n    34\tdocker-compose up -d\n    35\t\n    36\t# The service will be                      
                             available at:\n    37\t# http://localhost:8080/mcp\n    38\t\n    39\t# View logs\n                       
                             40\tdocker-compose logs -f\n    41\t\n    42\t# Stop the service\n    43\tdocker-compose                  
                             down\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\t## \ud83d\udc33 Docker Container Setup\n    47\t\n                   
                             48\t### Prerequisites\n    49\t\n    50\t- Docker and Docker Compose installed\n    51\t-                 
                             Node.js 20+ (for local development)\n    52\t\n    53\t### Build and Run\n    54\t\n                      
                             55\t#### Option 1: Docker Compose\n    56\t\n    57\t```bash\n    58\t# Build and start\n                 
                             59\tdocker-compose up -d\n    60\t\n    61\t# Check status\n    62\tdocker-compose ps\n                   
                             63\t\n    64\t# View logs\n    65\tdocker-compose logs -f vhl-library\n    66\t\n    67\t#                
                             Stop and remove\n    68\tdocker-compose down\n    69\t```\n    70\t\n    71\t#### Option 2:               
                             Docker CLI\n    72\t\n    73\t```bash\n    74\t# Build the image\n    75\tdocker build -t                 
                             vhl-library .\n    76\t\n    77\t# Run the container\n    78\tdocker run -d \\\n    79\t                  
                             --name vhl-library \\\n    80\t  -p 8080:8080 \\\n    81\t  -v vhl-lib:/app/lib \\\n    82\t              
                             -e VHL_TRANSPORT=http \\\n    83\t  -e PORT=8080 \\\n    84\t  vhl-library\n    85\t\n                    
                             86\t# Check health\n    87\tcurl http://localhost:8080/health\n    88\t\n    89\t# View                   
                             logs\n    90\tdocker logs -f vhl-library\n    91\t\n    92\t# Stop and remove\n    93\tdocker             
                             stop vhl-library && docker rm vhl-library\n    94\t```\n    95\t\n    96\t### Environment                 
                             Variables\n    97\t\n    98\t| Variable | Default | Description |\n                                       
                             99\t|----------|---------|-------------|\n   100\t| `VHL_TRANSPORT` | `stdio` | Transport                 
                             mode: `stdio` (local) or `http` (Docker) |\n   101\t| `PORT` | `8080` | HTTP server port                  
                             (only for http transport) |\n   102\t| `VHL_LIBRARY_DIR` | `/app/lib` | Directory for                     
                             persistent component storage |\n   103\t\n   104\t### Volume Persistence\n   105\t\n                      
                             106\tThe Docker container uses a named volume (`vhl-lib`) to persist components:\n   107\t\n              
                             108\t```bash\n   109\t# Inspect the volume\n   110\tdocker volume inspect vhl-lib\n   111\t\n             
                             112\t# Backup the volume\n   113\tdocker run --rm -v vhl-lib:/data -v $(pwd):/backup alpine               
                             tar czf /backup/vhl-backup.tar.gz -C /data .\n   114\t\n   115\t# Restore from backup\n                   
                             116\tdocker run --rm -v vhl-lib:/data -v $(pwd):/backup alpine tar xzf                                    
                             /backup/vhl-backup.tar.gz -C /data\n   117\t```\n   118\t\n   119\t## \ud83d\udce1 MCP Server             
                             API Reference\n   120\t\n   121\tThe VHL Library implements the Model Context Protocol (MCP)              
                             and exposes three tools via JSON-RPC 2.0.\n   122\t\n   123\t### Transport Modes\n   124\t\n              
                             125\t#### stdio Transport (Local Development)\n   126\t- Used by default when running                     
                             locally\n   127\t- Communication via stdin/stdout\n   128\t- Best for testing, CI, and local              
                             development\n   129\t\n   130\t#### HTTP Transport (Docker)\n   131\t- Enabled when                       
                             `VHL_TRANSPORT=http`\n   132\t- RESTful JSON-RPC endpoint at `POST /mcp`\n   133\t- Health                
                             check endpoint at `GET /health`\n   134\t- Best for production deployments and remote                     
                             access\n   135\t\n   136\t### Tool 1: `add_component`\n   137\t\n   138\tAdd or update a TSX              
                             component or KiCAD footprint in the local library.\n   139\t\n   140\t**Input Schema:**\n                 
                             141\t```json\n   142\t{\n   143\t  \"component_name\": \"string (required)\",\n   144\t                   
                             \"file_content\": \"string (required)\"\n   145\t}\n   146\t```\n   147\t\n                               
                             148\t**Parameters:**\n   149\t- `component_name`: Component name (e.g., `MyResistor`) or                  
                             filename with extension (e.g., `footprint.kicad_mod`)\n   150\t  - For TSX files: Name will               
                             have `.tsx` appended if not specified\n   151\t  - For KiCAD files: Must end with                         
                             `.kicad_mod`\n   152\t- `file_content`: Complete file content (TSX or KiCAD footprint                     
                             format)\n   153\t\n   154\t**Validation Rules:**\n   155\t- TSX files must be valid                       
                             TypeScript with JSX\n   156\t- TSX files must export at least one symbol\n   157\t- TSX files             
                             importing `.kicad_mod` files must have those footprints already in the library\n   158\t-                 
                             KiCAD `.kicad_mod` files are accepted without compilation validation\n   159\t\n                          
                             160\t**Response:**\n   161\t```json\n   162\t{\n   163\t  \"success\": true,\n   164\t                    
                             \"componentPath\": \"/app/lib/MyResistor.tsx\"\n   165\t}\n   166\t```\n   167\t\n                        
                             168\t**Error Response:**\n   169\t```json\n   170\t{\n   171\t  \"success\": false,\n   172\t             
                             \"errors\": [\n   173\t    \"Component must export at least one symbol\"\n   174\t  ]\n                   
                             175\t}\n   176\t```\n   177\t\n   178\t**Example (HTTP):**\n   179\t```bash\n   180\tcurl -X              
                             POST http://localhost:8080/mcp \\\n   181\t  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n                   
                             182\t  -d '{\n   183\t    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n   184\t    \"id\": 1,\n   185\t                         
                             \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n   186\t    \"params\": {\n   187\t      \"name\":                           
                             \"add_component\",\n   188\t      \"arguments\": {\n   189\t        \"component_name\":                   
                             \"MyResistor\",\n   190\t        \"file_content\": \"import { resistor } from                             
                             \\\"@tsci/seveibar.resistor\\\";\\n\\nexport const MyResistor = (props: { resistance: string              
                             }) => {\\n  return <resistor resistance={props.resistance} />;\\n};\"\n   191\t      }\n                  
                             192\t    }\n   193\t  }'\n   194\t```\n   195\t\n   196\t### Tool 2:                                      
                             `list_local_components`\n   197\t\n   198\tList all components in the local library.\n                    
                             199\t\n   200\t**Input Schema:**\n   201\t```json\n   202\t{}\n   203\t```\n   204\t\n                    
                             205\t**Response:**\n   206\t```json\n   207\t[\n   208\t  {\n   209\t    \"name\":                        
                             \"MyResistor\",\n   210\t    \"file\": \"MyResistor.tsx\"\n   211\t  },\n   212\t  {\n                    
                             213\t    \"name\": \"MyCapacitor\",\n   214\t    \"file\": \"MyCapacitor.tsx\"\n   215\t  }\n             
                             216\t]\n   217\t```\n   218\t\n   219\t**Example (HTTP):**\n   220\t```bash\n   221\tcurl -X              
                             POST http://localhost:8080/mcp \\\n   222\t  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n                   
                             223\t  -d '{\n   224\t    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n   225\t    \"id\": 2,\n   226\t                         
                             \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n   227\t    \"params\": {\n   228\t      \"name\":                           
                             \"list_local_components\",\n   229\t      \"arguments\": {}\n   230\t    }\n   231\t  }'\n                
                             232\t```\n   233\t\n   234\t### Tool 3: `search_library`\n   235\t\n   236\tSearch both local             
                             and global (tscircuit registry) libraries for components.\n   237\t\n   238\t**Input                      
                             Schema:**\n   239\t```json\n   240\t{\n   241\t  \"query\": \"string (required)\",\n   242\t              
                             \"mode\": \"fuzzy | regex (required)\",\n   243\t  \"depth\": \"surface | deep (required)\"\n             
                             244\t}\n   245\t```\n   246\t\n   247\t**Parameters:**\n   248\t- `query`: Search query                   
                             string\n   249\t- `mode`: \n   250\t  - `fuzzy`: Case-insensitive substring matching\n                    
                             251\t  - `regex`: Regular expression pattern matching\n   252\t- `depth`:\n   253\t  -                    
                             `surface`: Returns only name and source\n   254\t  - `deep`: Returns name, source, and                    
                             additional metadata (exports, pins)\n   255\t\n   256\t**Response (surface):**\n                          
                             257\t```json\n   258\t[\n   259\t  {\n   260\t    \"name\": \"MyResistor\",\n   261\t                     
                             \"source\": \"local\",\n   262\t    \"description\": \"Custom resistor component\"\n   263\t              
                             },\n   264\t  {\n   265\t    \"name\": \"seveibar.resistor\",\n   266\t    \"source\":                    
                             \"global\",\n   267\t    \"description\": \"Standard resistor - Stars: 42\"\n   268\t  }\n                
                             269\t]\n   270\t```\n   271\t\n   272\t**Response (deep):**\n   273\t```json\n   274\t[\n                 
                             275\t  {\n   276\t    \"name\": \"MyResistor\",\n   277\t    \"source\": \"local\",\n   278\t             
                             \"exports\": [\"default\"]\n   279\t  },\n   280\t  {\n   281\t    \"name\":                              
                             \"seveibar.resistor\",\n   282\t    \"source\": \"global\",\n   283\t    \"description\":                 
                             \"Standard resistor\",\n   284\t    \"exports\": [\"default\"]\n   285\t  }\n   286\t]\n                  
                             287\t```\n   288\t\n   289\t**Example (HTTP):**\n   290\t```bash\n   291\tcurl -X POST                    
                             http://localhost:8080/mcp \\\n   292\t  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n   293\t                
                             -d '{\n   294\t    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n   295\t    \"id\": 3,\n   296\t    \"method\":                 
                             \"tools/call\",\n   297\t    \"params\": {\n   298\t      \"name\": \"search_library\",\n                 
                             299\t      \"arguments\": {\n   300\t        \"query\": \"resistor\",\n   301\t                           
                             \"mode\": \"fuzzy\",\n   302\t        \"depth\": \"surface\"\n   303\t      }\n   304\t                   
                             }\n   305\t  }'\n   306\t```\n   307\t\n   308\t### List Available Tools\n   309\t\n                      
                             310\tQuery the server for all available tools:\n   311\t\n   312\t**Request:**\n                          
                             313\t```bash\n   314\tcurl -X POST http://localhost:8080/mcp \\\n   315\t  -H \"Content-Type:             
                             application/json\" \\\n   316\t  -d '{\n   317\t    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n   318\t                       
                             \"id\": 0,\n   319\t    \"method\": \"tools/list\"\n   320\t  }'\n   321\t```\n   322\t\n                 
                             323\t**Response:**\n   324\t```json\n   325\t{\n   326\t  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n   327\t                 
                             \"id\": 0,\n   328\t  \"result\": {\n   329\t    \"tools\": [\n   330\t      {\n   331\t                  
                             \"name\": \"add_component\",\n   332\t        \"description\": \"Add or update a TSX                      
                             component or KiCAD footprint (.kicad_mod) in the local library. Validation is automatic.\",\n             
                             333\t        \"inputSchema\": { ... }\n   334\t      },\n   335\t      {\n   336\t                        
                             \"name\": \"list_local_components\",\n   337\t        \"description\": \"List components                  
                             explicitly added to the local library.\",\n   338\t        \"inputSchema\": { ... }\n   339\t             
                             },\n   340\t      {\n   341\t        \"name\": \"search_library\",\n   342\t                              
                             \"description\": \"Search local and global libraries for components.\",\n   343\t                         
                             \"inputSchema\": { ... }\n   344\t      }\n   345\t    ]\n   346\t  }\n   347\t}\n                        
                             348\t```\n   349\t\n   350\t## \ud83e\udd16 LLM Agent Integration Guide\n   351\t\n                       
                             352\t### Overview\n   353\t\n   354\tVHL Library is designed to be used by LLM agents to                  
                             manage hardware components. The MCP protocol provides a standardized interface that agents                
                             can use to:\n   355\t\n   356\t1. Search for components in local and global libraries\n                   
                             357\t2. Add new components with automatic validation\n   358\t3. List existing local                      
                             components\n   359\t4. Manage component dependencies\n   360\t\n   361\t### Integration                   
                             Steps\n   362\t\n   363\t#### Step <response clipped><NOTE>Due to the max output limit, only              
                             part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched               
                             inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking                  
                             for.</NOTE>ponentName(generatedCode),\n   364\t      file_content: generatedCode\n   365\t                
                             });\n   366\t\n   367\t    // 5. Handle validation errors\n   368\t    if (!result.success)               
                             {\n   369\t      // Retry with fixed code\n   370\t      const fixedCode = await                          
                             this.fixValidationErrors(\n   371\t        generatedCode,\n   372\t        result.errors\n                
                             373\t      );\n   374\t      \n   375\t      return await callMCPTool('add_component', {\n                
                             376\t        component_name: this.extractComponentName(fixedCode),\n   377\t                              
                             file_content: fixedCode\n   378\t      });\n   379\t    }\n   380\t\n   381\t    return {                 
                             created: true, path: result.componentPath };\n   382\t  }\n   383\t}\n   384\t```\n   385\t\n             
                             386\t### Testing Your Agent\n   387\t\n   388\tUse the provided test suite as a reference:\n              
                             389\t\n   390\t```bash\n   391\t# Run MCP server tests\n   392\tpnpm test tests/mcp/\n                    
                             393\t\n   394\t# Run Docker integration tests\n   395\tpnpm test tests/docker/\n   396\t```\n             
